<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<worksheet>

  <title>Homework 07</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      <alert>Instructions:</alert> Complete the exercises and please aim to submit your work by the due date. As for the MATLAB Lab, create an .m file titled Lab7_YourLastName.m and  then compare your analytic results with
  graphical representations produced in MATLAB.
  You may also use MATLAB to double-check your calculations, but you must show your work.

            Solve each differential equation using the most appropriate method.
            Clearly identify the type of differential equation before solving.
            Show all work and verify your solutions whenever possible.
        </p>

    </introduction>

    <exercise workspace="1 in">
        <title>Separable Differential Equation</title>

        <p>Solve the differential equation.</p>

        <me>
            \frac{dy}{dx}
            =
            \frac{x^2+1}{y(y^2+4)}.
        </me>

    </exercise>

    <exercise workspace="1 in">
        <title>Linear First-Order Equation</title>

        <p>Solve the differential equation.</p>

        <me>
            (x^2+1)\frac{dy}{dx}+2xy=\sin x.
        </me>

    </exercise>

    <exercise workspace="1 in">
        <title>Bernoulli Equation</title>

        <p>Solve the differential equation.</p>

        <me>
            y'+\frac{2}{x}y=x^3y^{-2},
            \qquad x>0.
        </me>

    </exercise>

    <exercise workspace="1 in">
        <title>Distinct Real Roots</title>

        <p>Solve the differential equation.</p>

        <me>
            2y''+3y'-9y=0.
        </me>

    </exercise>

    <exercise workspace="1 in">
        <title>Repeated Roots</title>

        <p>Solve the differential equation.</p>

        <me>
            9y''-12y'+4y=0.
        </me>

    </exercise>

    <exercise workspace="1 in">
        <title>Complex Roots</title>

        <p>Solve the differential equation.</p>

        <me>
            y''+2y'+10y=0.
        </me>

    </exercise>

    <exercise workspace="1 in">
        <title>Fundamental Solutions and Wronskian</title>

        <p>
            Consider the differential equation
        </p>

        <me>
            y''+y=0.
        </me>

        <p>
            Verify that
        </p>

        <me>
            y_1=\cos x,
            \qquad
            y_2=\sin x
        </me>

        <p>
            are solutions of the differential equation.
            Compute the Wronskian and determine whether the functions form
            a fundamental set of solutions.
        </p>

    </exercise>

    <exercise workspace="1 in">
        <title>Undetermined Coefficients</title>

        <p>
            Find the general solution of
        </p>

        <me>
            y''-3y'+2y=(x+1)e^x.
        </me>

    </exercise>

    <exercise workspace="1 in">
        <title>Reduction of Order</title>

        <p>
            One solution of
        </p>

        <me>
            y''-4y'+4y=0
        </me>

        <p>
            is
        </p>

        <me>
            y_1=e^{2x}.
        </me>

        <p>
            Use reduction of order to find a second linearly independent
            solution and write the general solution.
        </p>

    </exercise>

    <exercise workspace="1 in">
        <title>Problem 10: Variation of Parameters</title>

        <p>
            Solve the differential equation using variation of parameters.
        </p>

        <me>
            y''+y=\sec x.
        </me>

    </exercise>
<subsection>
    <title>MATLAB Tips for Differential Equations</title>

    <p>
        MATLAB can help verify your work, perform symbolic integration,
        solve characteristic equations, and check solutions. The commands
        below use the Symbolic Math Toolbox.
    </p>

    <subsubsection>
        <title>Finding Characteristic Roots</title>

        <p>
            Example: Solve
        </p>

        <me>
            2r^2+3r-9=0.
        </me>

        <p>MATLAB commands:</p>

<cd>
syms r
solve(2*r^2 + 3*r - 9 == 0, r)
</cd>

        <p>
            MATLAB returns the characteristic roots.
        </p>

    </subsubsection>

    <subsubsection>
        <title>Symbolic Integration</title>

        <p>
            Example:
        </p>

        <me>
            \int x^2\ln(x)\,dx
        </me>

<cd>
syms x
int(x^2*log(x), x)
</cd>

        <p>
            MATLAB uses <m>log(x)</m> for the natural logarithm.
        </p>

    </subsubsection>

    <subsubsection>
        <title>Variation of Parameters Integrals</title>

        <p>
            Suppose variation of parameters requires
        </p>

        <me>
            u_1=-\int \ln(x)\,dx.
        </me>

<cd>
syms x
u1 = -int(log(x),x)
</cd>

        <p>
            MATLAB returns
        </p>

        <me>
            -x\ln(x)+x.
        </me>

    </subsubsection>

    <subsubsection>
        <title>Computing a Wronskian</title>

        <p>
            Given
        </p>

        <me>
            y_1=x^2,
            \qquad
            y_2=x^2\ln(x),
        </me>

        <p>
            the Wronskian can be computed directly.
        </p>

<cd>
syms x

y1 = x^2;
y2 = x^2*log(x);

W = det([y1 y2;
         diff(y1,x) diff(y2,x)]);

simplify(W)
</cd>

    </subsubsection>

    <subsubsection>
        <title>Checking a Solution</title>

        <p>
            Suppose you believe that
        </p>

        <me>
            y=C_1e^{-2t}\cos(4t)+C_2e^{-2t}\sin(4t)
        </me>

        <p>
            solves
        </p>

        <me>
            y''+4y'+20y=0.
        </me>

<cd>
syms t C1 C2

y = exp(-2*t)*(C1*cos(4*t)+C2*sin(4*t));

simplify(diff(y,t,2) + 4*diff(y,t) + 20*y)
</cd>

        <p>
            MATLAB should return zero if the solution is correct.
        </p>

    </subsubsection>

    <subsubsection>
        <title>Solving an ODE with dsolve</title>

        <p>
            Example:
        </p>

        <me>
            y''+4y'+20y=0,
            \qquad
            y(0)=1,
            \qquad
            y'(0)=0.
        </me>

<cd>
syms y(t)

Dy = diff(y,t);

ode = diff(y,t,2) + 4*diff(y,t) + 20*y == 0;

cond1 = y(0)==1;
cond2 = Dy(0)==0;

sol = dsolve(ode,[cond1 cond2])
</cd>

        <p>
            MATLAB returns the solution satisfying the initial conditions.
        </p>

    </subsubsection>
</subsection>

</worksheet>
